4.21 Top-priority rollup: 14 + 8 + 5 + 6 + 12 + 4 + 12 + 3 + 8 + 8 + 4
     + 5 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 2 + 4 + 12 = 125 modules. With the
     top-of-domain 2–3 fillers for AI/ML, bio, fin, quantum, robotics,
     signal, this fits inside the 100–150 budget.

================================================================
BEAT 5 — WAVE 2 WORK PLAN (<=150 modules)
================================================================

The following ordering is recommended for Wave 2 subagent work.
Each subagent owns one or more buckets and is given the file
naming convention + the 4-element Academy header template. The
ordering is by blast radius: a failure in bucket 1 cascades to
every other bucket, so it must be green before any other bucket
starts.

  BUCKET 1 — Crypto correctness gate (33 modules, 4 subagents)
      4.1  std/crypto (14 modules)             — subagent A
      4.2  std/crypto_advanced + pqc_nist (8)  — subagent B
      4.3  std/crypto_secrets (5)              — subagent C
      4.4  std/fhe_lattices + sec/crypto + zk  — subagent D
            (6 modules, last because fhe ops
             depend on crypto primitives from
             buckets A/B)

  BUCKET 2 — Math & algorithms (16 modules, 1 subagent)
      4.5  std/math (12)
      4.6  std/math_extended (4)
      Note: this bucket may produce compile errors on
      bigdecimal/matrix_inversion; see part 5.

  BUCKET 3 — Collections, hash, simd (15 modules, 1 subagent)
      4.7  std/collections (12)
      4.8  std/hash (3)
      Note: many of these depend on std/collections/result.oo and
      std/option.oo; if those don't compile, this bucket stalls.

  BUCKET 4 — Network & encoding (20 modules, 2 subagents)
      4.9  std/net (8)                — subagent A (CAP-bound tests)
      4.10 std/encoding (8)          — subagent B (pure, easier)
      4.11 std/compile/parsers/markup (4)    — subagent B (extend)

  BUCKET 5 — I/O, OS, DB (13 modules, 2 subagents)
      4.12 compression/io/text (5)   — subagent A
      4.13 std/os/fs (4)             — subagent B (CAP)
      4.14 std/data/db/* (4)              — subagent C

  BUCKET 6 — Compilers, runtime, concurrency (10 modules, 2 subagents)
      4.15 std/sync/actor (4)        — subagent A
      4.16 compilers + llvm (4)      — subagent B
      4.17 wasm (2)                  — subagent B (extend)

  BUCKET 7 — Domain tails (18 modules, 3 subagents)
      4.18 3d/graphics (2)           — subagent A
      4.19 sec/security (4)         — subagent A (extend)
      4.20 ai/bio/fin/quantum/robotics/signal (12, 2 each)
                                        — subagent B/C

  Total: 33 + 16 + 15 + 20 + 13 + 10 + 18 = 125 modules.
  Headroom: 25 spare slots (out of the 150 cap) for follow-up
  buckets or for re-tests of modules that fail in their first
  attempt.

  Subagent count: 12 subagents, each owns 5–20 modules, each
  expected to ship 1 smoke fixture per module (125 new
  fixtures/*_smoke.oo files).
